well a CNN crew reporting on the  protests in Minneapolis was arrested by  police live on camera look at this do  you I am under arrest sir why why am i  under arrest  that is reporter Omar Jimenez who was  showing demonstrators being arrested  live on TV he along with the production  team were then escorted away by officers  after having offered to set up in a  different location he had since been  released and spoke to the network about  his experience it did cross my mind  that what is really happening here and  the one thing that gave me a little bit  of comfort was that it happened on live  TV there's been this when you talk  within the community about let's just  say what's happened with George Floyd  there's always a discussion that what's  happening isn't new it's being filmed  and that speaks to the power of having  something happen on camera because you  can have people speak up for you without  you saying anything and that gave me a  little bit of comfort knowing that you  guys saw what was happening I was living  what was happening and the country was  seeing what was happening unfold in real  time right before their eyes you don't  have to doubt my story it's not filtered  in any sort of way you saw it for your  own eyes Boni Raddatz who's a the  advocacy director for the Committee to  Protect Journalists and joins us live  from Washington DC via Skype Courtney in  the context of the climate engendered by  the nation's leader by Donald Trump who  continually lambaste the fake news media  what do you make of the arrests of this  news team in these circumstances in  Minneapolis we're deeply shocked that  CNN reporter and his crew were arrested  live on air despite clearly identifying  themselves as members of the press  offering to comply with any police  requests to move location and this is  not acceptable in a democracy it's not  acceptable behavior by law enforcement  and of course we're concerned as we  recently reported in our latest report  on the Trump administration the media of  how this constant vilification and  harassment of the press by President  Trump and other political leaders  vilifies them and creates a permissive  environment where violence could become  more likely and frankly sends a signal  to law enforcement that hey you know CNN  is as Trump has called them the enemy of  the people  so you can't see any circumstances in  which these arrests could have been  justified based on what I saw in the  video and and you know what what we saw  the calm demeanor of these reporters the  very clear compliance with any you know  requests  no and the fact that they were not able  to verify that they were media just  seems ludicrous in light of the fact  that they were live-streaming  it wouldn't take an hour to you know  they didn't need to handcuff them it  wouldn't take an hour to verify they  could have gone on CNN and looked at the  livestream and see themselves on  television so that just does not ring  true and I think that the fact that you  heard the governor apologized clearly  indicates that no this was not  acceptable behavior and this should send  a signal more broadly to law enforcement  that they must protect the rights of  journalists to report freely and without  fear of reprisal or harassment or arrest  right it's also kind of curious or I  should say blatant really that you see a  black reporter they're being led away in  handcuffs arrested for doing his job  when a white member of the team was not  arrested  look we don't think any journalist  should be arrested for doing their jobs  but we also know that in this  environment that journalists of color  women journalists journalists who  somehow occupy a main minoritized  identity face additional harassment and  threats in doing their jobs according to  the u.s. press freedom tracker which we  helped found there have been at least  two arrests of journalists this year  nine arrests last year we saw for  example Standing Rock that Native  American journalists were arrested so  you know unfortunately I can't say you  know personally that this is super  surprising what was surprising is that  any journalists were being arrested in  America for doing their job calmly  complying with law enforcement there was  literally no excuse for that behavior on  the part of the Minneapolis Police  Department it's almost unbelievable look  at these  isn't it given the importance of this  story how concerned are you at the  ability for journalists to report what's  going on with these protests and then  knocking it forwards to November as we  enter the run in to the election we are  deeply concerned about the safety of  reporters reporting on the election on  protest on civil unrest and then you  wrap into that the challenges of  reporting during the corona virus  pandemic so in fact our cpj emergencies  which provides life-saving guidance and  support to journalists on the ground has  put out several safety advisories about  how to cover civil unrest safely how to  cover coronavirus we're working on a  whole election safety guide so this is  taking a lot of efforts a lot of  resources by us by other organizations  that are committed to ensuring that the  American public is able to be informed  during this important political period  which depends on journalists being able  to do their jobs safely and securely  record needed stay great to get your  perspective on this important story I  Cordelia right it's a committee to  protect journalists thanks very much  indeed thank you my pleasure Thanks
 

